---
description: How the runtime agents are built
globs: src/agent/agents/**,src/agent/prompts/**,src/agent/orchestrator.ts,src/agent/llm.ts,src/agent/sample-model.ts
alwaysApply: false
---
- One agent per file in src/agent/agents/. Its system prompt is the matching file in src/agent/prompts/, loaded as-is. Don't build prompts by string concatenation with data.
- The user message is one JSON object (JSON.stringify of a typed input). The reply is parsed with llm.ts's parseJsonReply (which strips reasoning blocks and code fences) and validated with a zod schema in the same file. llm.ts retries an unparseable reply once, then throws a typed BadJson error. On a zod failure, each agent does only what its step builds: the Planner drops each invalid step and traces it, never running it; the Writer retries once with retry_reason, then returns needs_human with the draft; the Analyst has no retry, and a zod failure ends its call as a structured error. Tool calling: the Analyst gets the task's named queries as native tools (OpenAI function calling) and may call them, at most MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS rounds; code runs every call through guards.beforeQuery, the cache and the budget, and traces it as action "tool_call". Every other agent replies with JSON only. The model never writes SQL.
- Models: the Planner and the Writer use config.models.strong; the Analyst uses config.models.main; the Evaluator's scoring uses config.models.fast; no reasoning switch goes to Nightshift (it makes the lane return empty replies), and a scoring call that returns no answer falls back to the code checks (score_unavailable). Each is a meterless/* routing id that GET <METERLESS_BASE_URL>/models lists (tier ids are routing modes (meterless/deep, auto, fast); Nightshift picks the model from its active lanes (Nemotron lanes only); a concrete model id in the request does not steer the route; two tiers may hold the same id). Every call goes to the endpoint in METERLESS_BASE_URL (the Nightshift Runtime URL), never to a hardcoded host, and sends the headers meterless-app: tradeflow and meterless-scope: user. Never hardcode a model id or a provider host. llm.ts falls back from "strong" to "main" when the strong model is unavailable, and traces it; "main" and "fast" don't fall back. A 401 or 403 is the typed error Unauthorized and a refused connection is NightshiftNotRunning; neither falls back to another model. Every call sends TEMPERATURE and MAX_COMPLETION_TOKENS from config.ts and gets the time left in the run as its timeout.
- Every agent receives the tracer and logs input_summary, output_summary, target_agent and confidence. Summaries are short and contain no secrets. Each planned step's why goes to the trace.
- Every agent must work with sample-model.ts, so tests and SAMPLE_MODE need no network. The sample model builds its reply from the input facts with the deterministic rules each step specifies; it never returns a stored answer.
- The orchestrator owns the limits from config.ts (planned queries, rounds, query calls, model calls, tokens, round and run time) and stops with status "needs_human", output.partial and output.stop_reason when one runs out. It adds up usage from every call. Query results are cached per run by query id and params: a retry round reuses them (traced as "cached", counted as no query call).
- Usage is written once per call: each model_call line carries usage.model_calls 1 and that call's tokens, each non-cached Query line carries usage.query_calls 1, agent summary and tool_call lines carry none, and the run_end line carries the total. A run's lines other than run_end add up to the reply's usage.
- Context: models get query rows through context.ts (at most 50 per query, with row_count and truncated; week_moves keeps the PORTFOLIO row first and the holdings by absolute contribution), at most 5 memories as id, tags, content and relevance, and nothing over MAX_PROMPT_CHARS. A step that adds data to a model's input (memories, world facts, rows) adds a test that runs the same task twice in one data dir and asserts every model call's system plus user stays within MAX_PROMPT_CHARS, because stored state grows between runs. The Evaluator and Writer see only the rows the findings cite. checkCitations reads the full rows from the run's result store. The Planner's catalogue is context.catalogueFor(task).
- Revise, don't restart: on rounds 2 and 3 the Planner gets the Evaluator's fix as feedback, and the Analyst gets its previous findings and the fix.
- The Evaluator runs code checks before the model: guards.checkCitations, guards.afterModel, then coverage (explain_week: the largest holding move has a finding; thesis_check: one verdict per thesis; pre_trade: a pre_trade finding; reconcile: a finding per issue_id). A failed check is a rejection with a one-sentence fix written by code. Its total out of 9, as a fraction rounded to two decimals (7 of 9 is 0.78), is the trace's confidence: 7 or more goes to the Writer, below 7 back to the Planner, still below 7 after round 3 is needs_human.
- The Writer gets the accepted findings with their cited rows, the gaps, the recalled memories and the applied corrections. Code checks its reply: every number appears in a finding's numbers, a cited row or a recalled memory; the last line is the disclaimer; no advice. One retry, then needs_human.
- Every reply carries output.rounds, output.scores, output.model_source (never a key or URL), output.memories_recalled, output.applied_corrections and usage.
- Prompt chaining: open questions (explain_week, thesis_check) are planned by the Planner; tasks whose evidence never changes (pre_trade, reconcile) are a fixed chain: code builds the plan, traces it as agent "Orchestrator", action "fixed_plan", skips the Planner, and sends a rejection back to the Analyst.
- Each system prompt in src/agent/prompts/ ends with short worked examples on a made-up holding (ACMEPHARMA). Keep them when editing a prompt; never use real Tradeflow figures in them.
- Briefing (A8): the "briefing" task is orchestration in code, not a new loop. It calls run() for explain_week, thesis_check and reconcile, each with its own limits and trace file, writes one Orchestrator "fixed_plan" line and one "handoff" line per child (with child_run_id), passes each child only the time left, and builds review_markdown in code with no model call. The children run up to MAX_CONCURRENT_CHILDREN at a time; sections and handoff lines stay in task order, written at fan-in. A child that stops never removes the other sections.
- One loop, several tasks: explain_week (A1), thesis_check (A2), pre_trade (A3), reconcile (A4). The task sets the Planner's catalogue; feedback (A5) is stored by code with no model loop. Branches that matter (a pre-trade check that would break a rule, a reconciliation proposal) are decided in code from the query rows, then traced, never left to the model.
